n></span></div><span class="Text Text__body3"><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/show/5600832240">April 25, 2024</a></span></section><section class="ReviewText"><section class="ReviewText__content" dir="auto"><div class="TruncatedContent" tabindex="-1"><div class="TruncatedContent__text TruncatedContent__text--large" tabindex="-1" data-testid="contentContainer"><span class="Formatted">This is the kind of book where you shouldn’t pause and think about what’s happening while you’re reading or it might ruin the experience for you when you realize that none of the world building or plot makes any sense. <br /><br />If you close one eye and take everything 100% at face value, I think there’s a good chance you can enjoy this book.<br /><br />It’s not *bad* per se but I do think lots of the book was constructed more for the ✨vibes✨ than anything substantive. <br /><br />The plot opens on a sort of “last supper” of twelve princess sisters dining with their dad, the king. The princesses have been locked away in the desert all their lives, training to become assassin spies. One of them will marry the King Aren of Ithicana, the enemy kingdom, and send all his secrets back to their father. <br /><br />So the king’s plan, now that it’s finally time for one princess to marry Aren, is to pick the best of his twelve surviving daughters (he started with twenty) and have the rest killed. <br /><br />This is a really stupid plan. What if your one chosen daughter catches the flu three days later and dies? Poof, there goes decades of your work. Shouldn’t you at least keep one spare on hand? Also, wtf does he even gain by killing the rest? If they haven’t spilled his secrets while trapped in the desert so far, why would they start now? <br /><br />Clearly this whole “kill the daughters” plot point is just for shock value and to make extra extra sure that the readers understand this dad is SUPER DUPER EVIL. <br /><br />Because, you know, forcing all your daughters to endure decades of torturous training in the desert then selling them off to your enemies isn’t evil enough. The silly readers might get confused and accidentally like this guy! <br /><br />So the FMC, Lara, is the princess who makes the cut and gets married off to Ithicana. She’s hellbent on finding a way to destroy her new husband and his kingdom by finding a gap in their defenses which will allow her dad’s army to invade and slaughter the Ithicanians. This, because she thinks Aren’s tough trade tax policies are causing her people back home to starve. <br /><br />Why does she believe this? Because her evil dad told her so. He told her he’s just so dedicated to helping his poor people who are suffering under those darn Ithicanian trade taxes. She takes this at full face value, since we all know evil child-murdering fathers are trustworthy and have only the most benevolent of intentions. <br /><br />Now, if you, unlike Lara, have more than two braincells, you might have guessed the big twist here already—it’s actually her dad whose shitty policies are leading their people to starve!! Shocking!! I expected so much better out of a child murderer! <br /><br />I figured this out at maybe 3-4 chapters in. For Lara, it takes 75% of the book. <br /><br />Sure, she grew up to some degree brainwashed, but she clearly knows enough to understand that the way she and her sisters were raised was wrong and fucked up. If you get that, how do you not take the tiniest step forward in logic to understand that the evil people who raised you might just be LIARS?!<br /><br />Spending most of the book watching Lara make stupid decisions based on stupid assumptions that <i>will lead to the massacre of the Ithicanian people she now rules</i> was a tad frustrating.<br /><br />The rest of the plot was equally predictable if you’re at all familiar with romantasy tropes. <br /><br />As for the romance, I could see how some people might enjoy it, but it wasn’t very meaningful to me. Aren, the love interest, might as well have been a cardboard box with some eyes and abs drawn on it. His one personality trait was being into Lara.<br /><br />So basically this was one big meh-fest for me. I might try book 2, I might not.</span></div><div class=""></div></div></section></section><section class="ReviewCard__tags"><ul class="CollapsableList"><span tabindex="-1"><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/39575951?shelf=anti-heroine" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium"><span class="Button__labelItem">anti-heroine</span></a><span tabindex="-1"> </span><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/39575951?shelf=contract-marriage" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium"><span class="Button__labelItem">contract-marriage</span></a><span tabindex="-1"> </span><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/39575951?shelf=enemies-to-lovers" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium"><span class="Button__labelItem">enemies-to-lovers</span></a></span><span tabindex="-1"></span> <div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium" aria-label="Show all items in the list"><span class="Button__labelItem">...more</span></button></div></ul></section><footer class="SocialFooter"><div data-testid="stats" class="SocialFooter__statsContainer"><div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--inline Button--medium Button--subdued"><span class="Button__labelItem">430 likes</span></button></div><div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--inline Button--medium Button--subdued"><span class="Button__labelItem">39 comments</span></button></div></div><div data-testid="actions"><div class="SocialFooter__action"><div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--inline Button--medium" aria-label="Review unliked. Press to like review"><span class="Button__labelItem"><i class="Icon LikeIcon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M14.4813282,6.41129383 L14.586127,6.97676447 C14.5964